WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.848
AI can be useful during remediation
because it can draft safer alternatives,

2
00:00:05.928 --> 00:00:10.288
suggest validation logic, explain
framework-specific APIs,

3
00:00:10.368 --> 00:00:14.235
or propose tests that exercise the
corrected behavior.

4
00:00:14.315 --> 00:00:17.770
That does not make generated code safe by
default.

5
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:23.527
Every proposed change should be reviewed
like any other security-sensitive code

6
00:00:23.607 --> 00:00:24.182
change.

7
00:00:24.262 --> 00:00:29.281
Confirm that it fixes the verified issue,
preserves intended behavior,

8
00:00:29.361 --> 00:00:34.379
handles failure paths, and does not
introduce a new weakness elsewhere.

9
00:00:34.459 --> 00:00:37.010
Good remediation is tied to context.

10
00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:42.520
Replacing an unsafe API, adding input
validation, enforcing authorization,

11
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:48.194
improving error handling, or changing
cryptography must match the application

12
00:00:48.274 --> 00:00:49.343
architecture.

13
00:00:49.423 --> 00:00:54.195
A generic fix may compile and still be
wrong for the service boundary,

14
00:00:54.275 --> 00:00:58.224
data model, performance requirement, or
user workflow.

15
00:00:58.304 --> 00:01:03.158
Ask AI to explain assumptions and
tradeoffs, not just provide a patch.

16
00:01:03.238 --> 00:01:06.611
Ask for minimal changes when stability
matters,

17
00:01:06.691 --> 00:01:12.203
and ask for refactoring options when the
surrounding design is part of the risk.

18
00:01:12.283 --> 00:01:14.422
Tests are part of remediation.

19
00:01:14.502 --> 00:01:19.767
Add cases for the vulnerable behavior, the
corrected behavior, edge cases,

20
00:01:19.847 --> 00:01:21.986
and expected rejection paths.

21
00:01:22.066 --> 00:01:23.547
When AI drafts tests,

22
00:01:23.627 --> 00:01:28.975
review whether they prove the security
property rather than only increasing

23
00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:29.795
coverage.

24
00:01:29.875 --> 00:01:32.508
Avoid blind copy-paste remediation.

25
00:01:32.588 --> 00:01:37.442
Generated changes should go through normal
review, static analysis,

26
00:01:37.522 --> 00:01:41.635
dependency checks, build validation, and
human approval.

27
00:01:41.715 --> 00:01:46.981
The best remediation workflow uses AI to
accelerate drafting while keeping

28
00:01:47.061 --> 00:01:49.940
engineering ownership where it belongs.
